{
  "image_name": "be25e21428c144e6e8a12a2b05417598.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several photocatalytic and optoelectronic roles of polymeric carbon nitride (PCN) by showing how light-generated electrons and holes are separated and then used in different hybrid systems. At the core is a simple band diagram: illumination excites electrons from the valence band to the conduction band across an energy gap of about 2.6 eV, leaving holes behind.\n\nThe other sketches show how this charge separation can be exploited. In one case, coupling PCN with platinum and a platinum oxide cocatalyst enables overall water splitting, with electrons driving hydrogen evolution and holes supporting oxygen formation. Another scheme combines PCN with Ag/Ag3PO4 in a Z-scheme p–n heterojunction, where charges are routed between the two semiconductors so that one side performs reduction, such as converting CO2 or H2O to reduced products, while the other oxidizes water to O2. A separate example uses ferrocene-modified carbon nitride to photo-oxidize benzene through reactive oxygen species intermediates. The figure also depicts a bulk heterojunction between PCN and nitrogen-doped graphene films that promotes charge transport and hydrogen production, and finally a layered PCN/graphene oxide structure that can be laser-written into reduced graphene oxide for memory-device applications.",
